---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ImageUtilityTool from '../components/ImageUtilityTool.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
const schemas = [
  {'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'WebApplication',name:'ConvertAnyFile Compress Image',url:`${SITE.origin}/compress-image/`,applicationCategory:'MultimediaApplication',operatingSystem:'Any',isAccessibleForFree:true,offers:{'@type':'Offer',price:'0',priceCurrency:'USD'},featureList:['Compress JPG, PNG and WebP images','Batch compression up to 50 images','JPEG and WebP quality control','Keep source format or change output format','Local browser processing','Original and result size comparison','ZIP batch download']},
  {'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'BreadcrumbList',itemListElement:[{'@type':'ListItem',position:1,name:'Home',item:`${SITE.origin}/`},{'@type':'ListItem',position:2,name:'Image Tools',item:`${SITE.origin}/image-tools/`},{'@type':'ListItem',position:3,name:'Compress Image',item:`${SITE.origin}/compress-image/`}]},
];
---
<SiteLayout title="Compress Image Online Free — JPG, PNG & WebP | ConvertAnyFile" description="Compress JPG, PNG and WebP images free in your browser. Adjust image quality, keep the source format or export JPG, WebP or PNG, compare file sizes, and batch download locally." schema={schemas} noindex>
<section class="converter-page-hero image-tool-page-hero"><div class="site-container converter-page-hero-inner"><div class="breadcrumbs converter-page-breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/image-tools/">Image Tools</a><span>/</span><span>Compress Image</span></div><span class="eyebrow converter-page-eyebrow">Compress Image · Preview</span><h1>Compress images online without uploading them</h1><p>Reduce JPG, PNG and WebP file sizes locally in your browser. Adjust quality, keep the original format or export a different web-friendly format, compare the result size, and download one image or a ZIP.</p></div></section>
<section class="image-tool-page-tool"><div class="site-container"><ImageUtilityTool mode="compress" /></div></section>
<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Quality control</span><h2>Compress for delivery, not by guessing a magic percentage.</h2><p>JPG and WebP respond directly to the quality control because they use lossy compression. A lower quality setting usually reduces file size more, but the best result depends on image detail and where the image will be used. PNG remains lossless, so a smaller PNG output is not guaranteed.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Compare real output size</strong><p>After processing, each row shows the original file size and the actual result size. Batch totals also show the before-and-after size so you can judge whether the chosen settings helped.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">How it works</span><h2>Compress images locally in four steps.</h2></div><ol class="content-checklist"><li>Add JPG, PNG or WebP images.</li><li>Choose a quality level and keep the source format or select JPG, WebP or PNG.</li><li>Compress the batch in the browser worker.</li><li>Compare the result sizes and download useful outputs individually or together as ZIP.</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="content-wide-section white"><div class="site-container"><div class="content-intro-grid"><div><span class="section-kicker">Format choices</span><h2>Compression and format conversion are related but not identical.</h2><p>Keeping the source format is simplest when compatibility is already correct. Exporting a photographic PNG to WebP or JPG may reduce file size substantially, but transparent pixels cannot be represented in JPG and are flattened to white by the image engine. Use the dedicated Image Converter when format compatibility—not file size—is the main goal.</p></div><div class="content-summary-card"><strong>Keep the original</strong><p>Compression creates another encoded copy. If an image is important, keep the source and use the compressed file as a delivery version for websites, forms, messages or other size-sensitive destinations.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="content-wide-section"><div class="site-container"><div class="section-heading wide"><span class="section-kicker">Batch limits</span><h2>Large batches still have browser safety boundaries.</h2><p>The preview accepts up to 50 images, 20 MB per file and 200 MB total input. Files over the limits, duplicates and unreadable images are reported instead of disappearing silently from the queue.</p></div><div class="related-grid"><a class="related-card" href="/resize-image/">Resize Image <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/image-metadata/">Image Metadata <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/image-converter/">Image Converter <span>→</span></a><a class="related-card" href="/image-tools/">All Image Tools <span>→</span></a></div></div></section>
</SiteLayout>
